WEBVTT

00:00:00.587 --> 00:00:01.390
Hello everyone.

00:00:01.632 --> 00:00:03.973
Today, we are talking about the fade,

00:00:03.923 --> 00:00:07.070
the cut that male clients ask for most these days.

00:00:07.579 --> 00:00:09.342
A good fade looks very soft,

00:00:09.292 --> 00:00:12.830
as if the hair color shifts gradually from light to dark.

00:00:13.234 --> 00:00:16.080
But a single horizontal line, or one patchy spot,

00:00:16.030 --> 00:00:18.990
and the client sees it in the mirror straight away.

00:00:19.456 --> 00:00:22.030
The hard part of a fade is not fast hands.

00:00:22.295 --> 00:00:24.110
It is understanding what you are doing.

00:00:24.747 --> 00:00:26.519
So, what is a fade?

00:00:27.107 --> 00:00:29.799
The hair gets gradually shorter from the bottom up.

00:00:30.148 --> 00:00:34.953
At the very bottom, close to the nape and around the ears, the hair is very short,

00:00:34.903 --> 00:00:36.519
sometimes down to the skin.

00:00:37.034 --> 00:00:41.879
Moving up, the hair gets longer, and longer, all the way to the top.

00:00:42.283 --> 00:00:44.999
Short hair shows more scalp, so it looks light.

00:00:45.428 --> 00:00:46.999
Longer hair looks darker.

00:00:47.133 --> 00:00:50.919
So the eye sees a smooth band of color, from light to dark.

00:00:51.167 --> 00:00:53.799
Our goal is for that transition to be seamless:

00:00:53.989 --> 00:00:55.959
no lines, no steps.

00:00:56.585 --> 00:00:58.208
There are three common heights.

00:00:58.387 --> 00:01:02.448
A low fade starts just above the ear, and curves down to the nape.

00:01:02.788 --> 00:01:04.528
Most of the sides still have hair.

00:01:04.854 --> 00:01:07.715
It is subtle, and suits people who work in an office,

00:01:07.665 --> 00:01:10.048
or who are trying a fade for the first time.

00:01:10.468 --> 00:01:11.328
A mid fade:

00:01:11.517 --> 00:01:15.968
the transition sits around temple level, between the ear and the parietal ridge.

00:01:16.076 --> 00:01:19.798
A high fade goes up close to the parietal ridge, or past it,

00:01:19.748 --> 00:01:21.328
where the head is widest.

00:01:21.753 --> 00:01:23.648
The sides are almost down to the skin;

00:01:23.997 --> 00:01:25.568
it looks bold and sharp.

00:01:25.916 --> 00:01:29.648
Every shop names them a little differently, so these are only reference points.

00:01:29.912 --> 00:01:33.888
What matters is that you and the client point to the same spot on the head.

00:01:34.505 --> 00:01:36.464
Two more terms that often get mixed up:

00:01:36.637 --> 00:01:38.304
skin fade and taper.

00:01:38.720 --> 00:01:42.064
A skin fade means the very bottom is shaved down to the skin.

00:01:42.407 --> 00:01:43.744
A taper is softer:

00:01:44.093 --> 00:01:47.850
only the hairline at the sideburns and at the nape is gradually shortened,

00:01:47.800 --> 00:01:49.904
and the rest of the sides keeps its hair.

00:01:50.249 --> 00:01:53.344
A taper suits clients who want a neat look without showing skin.

00:01:53.760 --> 00:01:56.784
When a client says, I want a fade, ask back:

00:01:56.945 --> 00:01:58.544
do you want to see skin at the bottom?

00:01:59.140 --> 00:02:01.023
Before cutting, read the head.

00:02:01.445 --> 00:02:03.743
Place your hands on it, and feel gently.

00:02:04.324 --> 00:02:08.063
Find the parietal ridge, the widest part of the head on each side.

00:02:08.407 --> 00:02:11.743
And the occipital bone, the part that sticks out a little at the back.

00:02:12.223 --> 00:02:13.423
Something very common:

00:02:13.772 --> 00:02:15.503
the back of the head is a bit flat.

00:02:15.847 --> 00:02:19.183
Push the fade too high, and that flat spot shows;

00:02:19.406 --> 00:02:20.463
the head looks dented.

00:02:20.649 --> 00:02:24.383
Keep some length above the occipital bone, and the head looks rounder.

00:02:24.790 --> 00:02:26.543
Then look closely at the scalp.

00:02:26.958 --> 00:02:28.063
Are there any scars?

00:02:28.406 --> 00:02:29.823
A skin fade will show them.

00:02:30.273 --> 00:02:31.582
Ask the client first;

00:02:31.770 --> 00:02:33.583
sometimes you leave it a little longer there.

00:02:34.044 --> 00:02:36.863
And look for whorls, at the crown and at the nape.

00:02:37.370 --> 00:02:41.923
When a short fade passes through a whorl, the hair lies in several directions,

00:02:41.873 --> 00:02:43.423
and easily ends up patchy.

00:02:44.040 --> 00:02:45.348
Now, the tools.

00:02:45.675 --> 00:02:48.068
On the clipper, there is a lever on the side.

00:02:48.410 --> 00:02:52.308
Open it, the blade moves back, and the hair is left a little longer.

00:02:52.652 --> 00:02:55.988
Close it, the blade moves forward, and the hair is shorter.

00:02:56.406 --> 00:03:00.468
The direction differs from machine to machine, so try it on your own clipper.

00:03:00.805 --> 00:03:04.388
The lever is exactly what gives you the lengths in between two guards.

00:03:04.633 --> 00:03:08.548
Many guard sets also have half sizes, for very small steps.

00:03:08.649 --> 00:03:12.868
A good fade comes from many small steps like this, not from one big jump.

00:03:13.483 --> 00:03:15.033
Here is the usual way to do it.

00:03:15.458 --> 00:03:19.753
First, set a guideline with a guard, all around the head, at the chosen height.

00:03:20.342 --> 00:03:23.593
Then divide the area below that line into bands.

00:03:23.942 --> 00:03:25.673
The bottom band is the shortest.

00:03:26.021 --> 00:03:29.033
Each band going up uses a slightly longer length.

00:03:29.517 --> 00:03:32.473
At each stopping point, don't pull the clipper straight out.

00:03:32.803 --> 00:03:36.259
Flick your wrist, and scoop the clipper outward in a small curve,

00:03:36.209 --> 00:03:37.673
like scooping with a spoon.

00:03:37.911 --> 00:03:40.633
That way, the stopping point doesn't become a line.

00:03:41.205 --> 00:03:45.449
However careful you are, there is often still a faint line between two bands.

00:03:45.749 --> 00:03:46.569
To erase it:

00:03:46.918 --> 00:03:50.942
use the length in between those two bands, with the lever half open,

00:03:50.892 --> 00:03:52.169
or a half-size guard,

00:03:52.430 --> 00:03:55.049
and go only along the line, then flick out.

00:03:55.451 --> 00:03:57.049
Do not go over the whole area.

00:03:57.234 --> 00:03:58.569
Only where the line is.

00:03:58.991 --> 00:04:03.528
A very common mistake is being so afraid of lines that you keep going higher,

00:04:03.478 --> 00:04:04.169
and higher.

00:04:04.332 --> 00:04:07.289
The fade creeps up, higher than you promised the client.

00:04:07.476 --> 00:04:09.769
Stop, and erase the line where it is.

00:04:09.876 --> 00:04:10.649
Don't move it up.

00:04:11.277 --> 00:04:13.068
Lighting matters more than you think.

00:04:13.325 --> 00:04:17.228
Under light from above, lines and patches show up very clearly.

00:04:17.603 --> 00:04:22.028
In dim light, it looks smooth, and the client only sees the problem out in the sun.

00:04:22.309 --> 00:04:23.468
So check in good light.

00:04:23.877 --> 00:04:27.189
Tilt the client's head, look from the left, from the right,

00:04:27.139 --> 00:04:29.788
crouch down to eye level, and look at the back.

00:04:30.181 --> 00:04:31.388
Compare both sides:

00:04:31.656 --> 00:04:33.068
is the fade at the same height?

00:04:33.335 --> 00:04:37.148
Place a finger on each side to compare, just like checking sideburns.

00:04:37.749 --> 00:04:41.215
For a skin fade, the bottom part, down to the skin,

00:04:41.165 --> 00:04:44.364
is usually done with a foil shaver, or a razor.

00:04:44.709 --> 00:04:46.844
A foil shaver is gentler on the skin.

00:04:47.268 --> 00:04:51.662
With a razor, use only single-use blades, a new blade for every client,

00:04:51.612 --> 00:04:53.804
as in the lesson on cleaning tools.

00:04:54.232 --> 00:04:57.884
Sensitive skin easily gets red bumps or spots after shaving.

00:04:58.308 --> 00:05:00.993
Stretch the skin lightly, shave with the grain,

00:05:00.943 --> 00:05:03.404
and don't go over the same spot many times.

00:05:03.664 --> 00:05:07.932
If the client is prone to irritation, stop at the shortest clipper length,

00:05:07.882 --> 00:05:08.844
and don't shave.

00:05:09.273 --> 00:05:13.728
And if the scalp has a wound, is inflamed, or has pustules, don't shave,

00:05:13.678 --> 00:05:16.604
as in the lesson on when not to treat a client.

00:05:17.203 --> 00:05:19.093
Finally, tell the client up front.

00:05:19.501 --> 00:05:21.973
A fade looks its best in the first week or two.

00:05:22.389 --> 00:05:27.013
Hair grows about a centimeter a month, so the part close to the skin soon grows out.

00:05:27.520 --> 00:05:31.333
Many clients keep their fade by coming back after about two to three weeks.

00:05:31.683 --> 00:05:33.973
That is a common habit, not a rule;

00:05:34.322 --> 00:05:36.293
it depends on how sharp the client likes it.

00:05:36.640 --> 00:05:37.893
Write it in the client record:

00:05:38.138 --> 00:05:41.733
high or low fade, which guards, how the lever was set.

00:05:42.145 --> 00:05:45.093
Next time, whoever cuts can do exactly the same again.

00:05:45.739 --> 00:05:48.692
So you see, a great fade is not about speed.

00:05:49.190 --> 00:05:52.655
It is about choosing the right height, reading the head correctly,

00:05:52.605 --> 00:05:54.052
and working in small steps.

00:05:54.301 --> 00:05:58.772
In the practice lesson, we will cut a mid fade, step by step, together.

00:05:59.036 --> 00:06:01.972
And now, please take the short quiz below the video.

00:06:02.315 --> 00:06:03.092
See you next time.
